   215	free to post and accept offers that yield negative profits.}. We use 11 buyers
   216	and 11 sellers with reservation prices ranging from \$0.75 to \$3.25 in \$0.25
   217	increments, producing symmetric, step-function demand and supply schedules
   218	that intersect at a competitive equilibrium price $p^\star = \$2.00$ and equilibrium
   219	quantity $q^\star = 6$ trades per round (Figure \ref{fig:market-equilibrium}). 
   220	
   221	This intersection defines the \emph{competitive equilibrium}: the price at which
   222	the quantity buyers are willing to purchase equals the quantity sellers are willing
   223	to supply, and at which total surplus -- the sum of realized buyer and seller
   224	rents -- is maximized. Crucially, $p^\star$ is a prediction derived from the
   225	full demand and supply schedules, yet no individual agent observes these
   226	schedules or is told that \$2.00 is the equilibrium price; each agent knows
   227	only its own reservation value. The central finding of decades of experimental economic research shows that decentralized trading among such privately-informed,
   228	self-interested agents drives transaction prices toward this competitive prediction over repeated rounds \citep{davis1993experimental}. A market-level equilibrium thus emerges from purely local behavior.
   229	
